====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# [[elongated|Elongated]] or [[trailing]] portion.
## The [[elongated]] back portion of a [[dress]] or [[skirt]] (or an ornamental piece of material added to similar effect), which drags along the ground. {{defdate|from 14th c.}}
##: {{ux|en|Unfortunately, the leading bridesmaid stepped on the bride's '''train''' as they were walking down the aisle.}}
##* {{RQ:Austen Northanger Abbey|text=They called each other by their Christian name, were always arm in arm when they walked, pinned up each other's '''train''' for the dance, and were not to be divided in the set [...].}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1819|author=[[s:Author:Washington Irving|Washington Irving]]|title=[[s:The Sketchbook of Geoffrey Crayon|The Sketch Book]]|section=[[s:The Sketchbook of Geoffrey Crayon (1819)/Rip Van Winkle| Rip Van Winkle]]| passage=He was generally seen trooping like a colt at his mother's heels, equipped in a pair of his father's cast-off galligaskins, which he had much ado to hold up with one hand, as a lady does her '''train''' in bad weather.}}
##* {{quote-journal|en|author=Imogen Fox|journal=The Guardian|date=20 Apr 2011
|passage=Lace sleeves, a demure neckline, a full skirt and a relatively modest '''train'''.}}
## A [[trail]] or [[line]] of something, especially [[gunpowder]]. {{defdate|from 15th c.}}
##* {{quote-text|en|year=1785|author=w:Thomas Jefferson|url=http://tjrs.monticello.org/letter/1632|title=Letter to Richard Price
|passage=[E]mancipation is put into such a '''train''' that in a few years there will be no slaves Northward of Maryland.}}
##* {{quote-text|en|year=1873|author=w:Charlotte Mary Yonge|title=Aunt Charlotte's Stories of English History for the little ones
|passage=A party was sent to search, and there they found all the powder ready prepared, and, moreover, a man with a lantern, one Guy Fawkes, who had undertaken to be the one to set fire to the '''train''' of gunpowder, hoping to escape before the explosion.}}
## The [[tail]] of a [[bird]].
##* {{RQ:Shakespeare Henry 6-1|III|iii|108|2|passage=Let frantike ''Talbot'' triumph for a while,<br>And like a Peacock ſweepe along his tayle,<br>Wee’le pull his Plumes, and take away his '''Trayne''',<br>If Dolphin and the reſt will be but rul’d.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|title=Wandering Words |author=Sir Edwin Arnold |year=1894 |page=260 |passage=The burning evening sun lighted with mellow gold the coats of the fierce little tiger-kittens — orange silk with stripes of black velvet — the broken amethysts and ruined emeralds of the poor bird's '''train''' cruelly scattered over the trampled grass}}
##* {{quote-book|en|title=Australia in the making |author=William Henry Fitchett |year=1917 |page=xii |passage=Fawn and pearl of the lyre-bird's '''train''', / Sheen of the bronze-wing, blue of the crane; / Cream of the plover, grey of the dove; / These are the hues of the land I love!}}
##* {{quote-book|en|title=Nature Magazine |year=1945 |page=299 |passage=Before the Spanish Conquest, the long, slender, green plumes of the male bird's '''train''' adorned the headgear of Aztec and Mayan kings and chieftains, as one may clearly see in modern restorations of ancient scenes. }}
## {{lb|en|obsolete}} The tail of an [[animal]] in general.
## {{lb|en|poetic}} The elongated body or form of something [[narrow]] and [[winding]], such as the [[course]] of a [[river]] or the body of a [[snake]].
## {{lb|en|astronomy}} A [[transient]] trail of [[glow]]ing [[ion]]s behind a large [[meteor]] as it falls through the [[atmosphere]] or [[accompany]]ing a [[comet]] as it [[near]]s the sun; [[tail]].
##* {{quote-book|en|title=w:The Conversation of Eiros and Charmion |author=w:Edgar Allan Poe |year= 1839 |passage=Finally, all men saw that astronomical knowledge lied not, and they awaited the comet. Its approach was not, at first, seemingly rapid; nor was its appearance of very unusual character. It was of a dull red, and had little perceptible '''train'''. }}
##* {{quote-book|en|title=The World of Comets |author=Amédée Guillemin; James Glaisher |year=1877 |page=200 |passage=It sometimes happens that the '''train''' is directed towards the sun, or makes a certain angle with the line joining the head and the sun; it was then called by the ancient astronomers the ''beard'' of the comet. }}
##* {{quote-book|en|title=Popular Astronomy|author=Camille Flammarion |year=2014 |page=515| ISBN=1108067840 |passage=...the comet expands, its vapours are developed and escape in jets towards the radiant star; then we see them driven back on each side of the head and the caudal '''train''' commencing.}}
## {{lb|en|now|rare}} An [[animal]]'s [[trail]] or [[track]]. {{defdate|from 16th c.}}
## {{lb|en|obsolete|hunting}} Something [[drag]]ged or laid along the ground to form a trail of [[scent]] or [[food]] along which to [[lure]] an animal.
## {{lb|en|obsolete}} [[gait|Gait]] or [[manner]] of [[running]] of a [[horse]].
# Connected sequence of people or things.
## A group of [[people]] following an important [[figure]] such as a [[king]] or [[noble]]; a [[retinue]], a group of [[retainer]]s. {{defdate|from 14th c.}}
##* {{RQ:Shakespeare Tempest|5|1|text=Sir, I invite your Highness and your '''train'''&nbsp;/ To my poor cell, where you shall take your rest&nbsp;/For this one night}}
##* {{quote-book
|en
|year=1979
|author=Silas H. L. Wu
|title=Passage to Power: Kʻang-hsi and His Heir Apparent, 1661-1722
|url=https://archive.org/details/passagetopowerka0000wusi/
|publisher=w:Harvard University Press
|ISBN=0-674-65625-3
|LCCN=79-4191
|OCLC=251837794
|page=78
|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/passagetopowerka0000wusi/page/78/
|text=The imperial '''train''' arrived on November 22 at Te-chou, a city in western Shantung along the border of Chihli.}}
##* {{quote-text|en|year=2009|author=w:Anne Easter Smith|title=The King's Grace
|passage=Grace was glad the citizenry did not know Katherine Gordon was in the king’s '''train''', but she was beginning to understand Henry’s motive for including the pretender’s wife.}}
## A [[group]] of animals, vehicles, or people that [[follow]] one another in a [[line]], such as a wagon train; a [[caravan]] or [[procession]]. {{defdate|from 15th c.}}
##: {{ux|en|Our party formed a '''train''' at the funeral parlor before departing for the burial.}}
## {{lb|en|figuratively|poetic}} A [[group]] or [[class]] of people.
##* {{RQ:Marlowe Tamburlaine|part=1|scene=iii|page=49|passage=Theſe are the cruel pirates of ''Argier'',<br>That damned '''train''', the ſcum of ''Affrica'', {{...}}}}
## {{lb|en|military}} The men and vehicles following an [[army]], which carry [[artillery]] and other equipment for [[battle]] or [[siege]]. {{defdate|from 16th c.}}
## A [[sequence]] of events or ideas which are interconnected; a [[course]] or [[procedure]] of something. {{defdate|from 15th c.}}
##* {{quote-text|en|year=1872|author=w:Charles Darwin|title=The Expression of the Emotions in Man and Animals
|passage=A man may be absorbed in the deepest thought, and his brow will remain smooth until he encounters some obstacle in his '''train''' of reasoning, or is interrupted by some disturbance, and then a frown passes like a shadow over his brow.}}
##* {{quote-journal|en|year=1960|month=November|author=P. Ransome=Wallis|title=Modern motive power of the German Federal Railway: Part Three|journal=Trains Illustrated|page=679|text=Failure to acknowledge an A.T.C. warning or excessive speed starts the same '''train''' of events until correction is made.}}
##* {{quote-journal|en|author=Rory Carroll|journal=The Guardian|date=18 Jun 2012
|passage="Where was I?" he asked several times during the lunch, losing his '''train''' of thought.}}
## A set of things, events, or circumstances that follow after or as a [[consequence]]; [[aftermath]], [[wake]].
##* {{quote-book|en|title=s:Labour and Childhood|author=Margaret McMillan|year=1907|page=[[s:Page:Labour and childhood.djvu/146|120]]
|passage=Thus the development of reason is accompanied by no inner blight or withering. It does not bring in its '''train''' loss of faith or weakening of sympathies.}}
## {{lb|en|obsolete}} [[state|State]] of [[progress]], [[status]], [[situation]] (in phrases introduced by ''in a'' + adjective); {{ngd|also}} [[proper]] [[order]] or [[situation]] (introduced by ''in'' or ''in a'' alone). {{defdate|18th|19th c.}}
##: {{ux|en|in a fair / better / worse '''train'''}}
##* {{RQ:Richardson Clarissa|volume=4|letter=26|page=139|url=http://name.umdl.umich.edu/004835420.0001.004|text=As we had been in a good '''train''' for several days past, I thought it not prudent to break with him, for little matters.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1779|author=w:Samuel Jackson Pratt|title=Shenstone-Green: or, the New Paradise Lost|location=London|publisher=R. Baldwin|volume=1|chapter=7|page=46|url=http://name.umdl.umich.edu/004834350.0001.001
|passage=I took care that my absence should neither be lamented by the poor nor the rich. I put every thing in a fair '''train''' of going on smoothly, and actually set out, with my steward, for my estate in Wales at dawning of the day.}}
##* '''1787''', {{w|George Washington}}, letter to {{w|Alexander Hamilton}} dated 10{{nbsp}}July, 1787, in ''The Writings of George Washington,'' Boston: American Stationers’ Company, 1837, Volume{{nbsp}}9, p.{{nbsp}}260,<sup>[https://babel.hathitrust.org/cgi/pt?id=njp.32101072313156&view=1up&seq=272]</sup>
##*: When I refer you to the state of the counsels, which prevailed at the period you left this city, and add that they are now if possible in a worse '''train''' than ever, you will find but little ground on which the hope of a good establishment can be formed.
##* {{RQ:Austen Mansfield Park|III|VI|pages=120-121|pageref=121|url=https://archive.org/details/mansfieldparknov03aust/page/120|text={{...}} every thing was now in a fairer '''train''' for Miss Crawford’s marrying Edmund than it had ever been before.}}
## A set of interconnected [[mechanical]] parts which operate each other in [[sequence]]. {{defdate|from 18th c.}}
## A series of electrical pulses. {{defdate|from 19th c.}}
## A series of specified [[vehicle]]s (originally [[tramcar]]s in a [[mine]] as usual, later especially [[railway carriage]]s) coupled together. {{defdate|from 19th c.}}
## A mechanical (traditionally steam-powered, now typically [[diesel]] or electrical) vehicle carrying a large number of passengers and [[freight]] along a designated track or path; a [[line]] of connected [[wagon]]s considered overall as a mode of [[transport]]; (as uncountable noun) [[rail]] or [[road]] travel. {{defdate|from 19th c.}}
##: {{ux|en|The '''train''' will pull in at midday.}}
##* {{RQ:Churchill Celebrity|chapter=5|passage=We expressed our readiness, and in ten minutes were in the station wagon, rolling rapidly down the long drive, for it was then after nine.{{...}}As we reached the lodge we heard the whistle, and we backed up against one side of the platform as the '''train''' pulled up at the other.}}
##* {{quote-journal|en|author=w:Hanif Kureishi|journal=The Guardian|date=24 Jan 2009
|passage=This winter we thought we'd go to Venice by '''train''', for the adventure.}}
##* {{quote-journal|en|date=2013-06-01|volume=407|issue=8838| page=13 (Technology Quarterly)| magazine=w:The Economist| title=[http://www.economist.com/news/technology-quarterly/21578516-transport-new-train-technologies-are-less-visible-and-spread-less-quickly Ideas coming down the track]| passage=A “moving platform” scheme{{...}}is more technologically ambitious than maglev '''trains''' even though it relies on conventional rails. Local '''trains''' would use side-by-side rails to roll alongside intercity '''trains''' and allow passengers to switch '''trains''' by stepping through docking bays.}}
## {{lb|en|informal}} A [[service]] on a [[railway]] [[line]].
## A long, heavy [[sleigh]] used in [[Canada]] for the [[transportation]] of [[merchandise]], [[wood]], etc.
## {{lb|en|computing}} A [[software]] [[release]] [[schedule]].
##* {{quote-text|en|year=2008|author=Michael Bushong; Cathy Gadecki; Aviva Garrett|title=JUNOS For Dummies|page=16
|passage=What steps do development engineers follow when adding new feature code? How do they support different software versions or release '''trains'''?}}
## {{lb|en|sex|slang}} An act wherein series of men line up and then [[penetrate]] a person, especially as a form of [[gang rape]]. {{defdate|from 20th c.}}
##* {{quote-text|en|year=2005|author=Violet Blue|title=Best Women's Erotica 2006: Volume 2001|sectionurl=http://books.google.com/books?id=RFXkLXxWXh4C&pg=PT35&dq=run+a+train+cunt&hl=en&ei=6FLSTt22GOKdiALgwu2_Cw&sa=X&oi=book_result&ct=result&resnum=6&ved=0CEkQ6AEwBQ|section=link
|passage=“You want us to run a '''train''' on you?”}}

