====Verb====
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|transitive|obsolete}} To draw by persuasion, artifice, or the like; to attract by stratagem; to entice; to allure.
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare King John|act=III|scene=iv|passage=If but a dozen French / Were there in arms, they would be as a call / To '''train''' ten thousand English to their side.}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Comedy of Errors|act=III|scene=ii|passage=O, '''train''' me not, sweet mermaid, with thy note.}}
#* {{RQ:John Ford Whore|sig=I2|verso=1|page=69|passage=O doe not goe, this feaſt (I'le gage my life) / Is but a plot to '''trayne''' you to your ruine, / Be rul'd, you ſha'not goe.}}
#* {{RQ:Scott Talisman|passage=Thou hast been '''trained''' from thy post by some deep guile — some well-devised stratagem&nbsp;– the cry of some distressed maiden has caught thine ear, or the laughful look of some merry one has taken thine eye.}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete|colloquial}} To be on intimate terms with. <!--Chambers 1908-->

